# PawFeed

A cat food dispenser that recognizes who is coming: it only opens for the
cats and stays closed for the dog.

## Why this project?

At home I have two cats, Kait and Noisette, and a dog, Skiff.
The problem: Skiff eats the cats' food. I wanted a dispenser that can tell
them apart on its own, with no human involved.

## How does it work?

1. An infrared camera (day and night) watches the area in front of the
   dispenser.
2. A Raspberry Pi 4 analyzes the image with an AI model that recognizes
   a cat or a dog.
3. If it's a cat, a stepper motor turns a 3D-printed auger that pushes
   the food toward the bowl.
4. If it's a dog, or if the model is not sure, nothing comes out.
5. A web page shows the live status: green for a cat, red for a dog,
   gray when there is nothing.

## What I used

- Raspberry Pi 4 (1 GB) running Raspberry Pi OS Lite 64-bit
- Arducam IR-CUT day/night camera (OV5647, 5 MP)
- 28BYJ-48 stepper motor with a ULN2003 driver
- 3D-printed parts (hopper, auger, body) made on an
  Elegoo Centauri Carbon 2 Combo
- TensorFlow Lite AI model trained on the public Cats vs Dogs dataset
  (Microsoft/Kaggle)
- Python, Flask

## What I gave up along the way

- An RFID chip to recognize the cats: replaced by the camera.
- A weight sensor to measure portions: dropped, calibration was too
  unstable.
- A trapdoor, then a disc system: replaced by the auger.

